From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:17:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: schedutil: Let the driver set where
 down_hold_us starts

down_hold_us always starts at 0, so a platform that needs the hold
has to write it from user space after every boot.

Add a down_hold_us field to struct cpufreq_policy that the scaling
driver may set. schedutil takes the tunable's starting value from it,
as it already takes rate_limit_us from transition_delay_us. Every
driver leaves it at 0, so nothing changes.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 include/linux/cpufreq.h          | 6 ++++++
 kernel/sched/cpufreq_schedutil.c | 1 +
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/linux/cpufreq.h b/include/linux/cpufreq.h
index d3d0d9d02a..5784bba40e 100644
--- a/include/linux/cpufreq.h
+++ b/include/linux/cpufreq.h
@@ -131,6 +131,12 @@ struct cpufreq_policy {
 	 */
 	unsigned int		transition_delay_us;
 
+	/*
+	 * Starting value of the schedutil down_hold_us tunable, in us (0, the
+	 * default, means no hold). To be set by the scaling driver.
+	 */
+	unsigned int		down_hold_us;
+
 	/*
 	 * Remote DVFS flag (Not added to the driver structure as we don't want
 	 * to access another structure from scheduler hotpath).
diff --git a/kernel/sched/cpufreq_schedutil.c b/kernel/sched/cpufreq_schedutil.c
index a3cd24cba9..b1ada802c6 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/cpufreq_schedutil.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/cpufreq_schedutil.c
@@ -846,6 +846,7 @@ static int sugov_init(struct cpufreq_policy *policy)
 	}
 
 	tunables->rate_limit_us = cpufreq_policy_transition_delay_us(policy);
+	tunables->down_hold_us = policy->down_hold_us;
 
 	policy->governor_data = sg_policy;
 	sg_policy->tunables = tunables;
-- 
2.43.0

